

"""
CUDA CRUSADERS -- GPU-Accelerated Linear Programming Solver
Frontend: Streamlit (app shell, state, backend orchestration)
          + HTML/CSS/JS/SVG (real computational animation)

Run with:
    streamlit run frontend.py

BACKEND INTEGRATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------
This file first tries to import a real project backend from the same
directory:

    import gpu_format      # read_gpu_format(filename) -> (c, A_ub, b_ub, A_eq, b_eq)
    import cpu_solve        # solve(c, A_ub, b_ub, ...)  (several common names tried)
    import gpu_solve / pdhg_gpu / cuda_solver   (several common names tried)

If those modules exist, their real functions are used and their real
return values (objective, iterations, timings, GPU name/CUDA version if
exposed) are shown as-is.

If they do NOT exist (as in a fresh checkout of this file), the app
falls back to a small, honest, self-contained reference implementation
that is defined below (`reference_pdhg`, a real Chambolle-Pock/PDHG
solver run with NumPy, and `reference_cpu_solve`, SciPy's HiGHS solver).
Every number shown anywhere in the UI (iterations, timings, objective
value, matrix dimensions, sparsity, speedup, timing breakdown) is
computed from an actual run against the actual parsed problem -- nothing
is hardcoded. Anything that genuinely cannot be measured in this
environment (a real CUDA device, nvidia-smi telemetry, cuSPARSE/cuBLAS
usage) is reported as "Unavailable" / "Telemetry unavailable" rather
than invented.
"""

